# N1 — the CPU step's single spike buffer, and its fix

Review item N1 on PR #246: `step_cpu` used one `state.spike` vector as both the
previous tick's input and this tick's output, so a neuron whose presynaptic
partner had a lower index read that partner's *current* tick spike. The update
was sequential (Gauss-Seidel), not the synchronous recurrence the module doc
claimed and the kernel performs. Fixed by a second buffer and a swap, exactly as
`gpu.rs` already does.

## The demonstration

Throwaway driver at `C:/tmp/Impl246Fixes/aliasdemo/` (not committed). It compiles
two modules side by side with `rustc -O --edition 2021`:

* `old_lif.rs` — `git show 7c09881:crates/connectome-cns/src/lif.rs`
* `new_lif.rs` — the fixed worktree file

(only the leading `//!` module doc is turned into `//` so `include!` accepts it;
every code line is the literal source).

Input: 2 neurons, one edge **neuron 0 -> neuron 1** (excitatory, weight 1) — so
neuron 1's presynaptic partner has the **lower index**, the case the old buffer
aliased. Params `decay 0.9, threshold 1.0, reset 0.0, refractory 0`. Tick 0 is
driven with `external = [1.0, 0.0]`; ticks 1-3 are free-running.

```
graph: 2 neurons, edge 0 -> 1 (+1, weight 1); params decay 0.9 threshold 1.0 reset 0.0 refractory 0
tick 0 external = [1.0, 0.0]; ticks 1-3 external = []

tick 0: single-buffer(7c09881) fired [0, 1]   double-buffered(HEAD) fired [0]
tick 1: single-buffer(7c09881) fired []   double-buffered(HEAD) fired [1]
tick 2: single-buffer(7c09881) fired []   double-buffered(HEAD) fired []
tick 3: single-buffer(7c09881) fired []   double-buffered(HEAD) fired []

the first tick differs: the single buffer fires neuron 1 one tick early (it read neuron 0's spike written earlier in the same pass);
the double-buffered path defers it to tick 1. total_spikes: 7c09881 2 vs fixed 2
```

Neuron 0 crosses threshold at tick 0 and fires; with one buffer, neuron 1 is
processed after it and reads the spike that was written microseconds earlier in
the same pass, so it fires at tick 0. The fixed path reads the previous tick's
zero and fires at tick 1: the spike moves one tick later, which is the
order-dependence the review named. A free-running bench cannot show this: with no
external current no neuron ever fires, so `spikes on the last tick` is 0 and the
two paths agree trivially.

## Why the headline numbers are unaffected

The kernel path (`kernels/cns_lif.cu`, driven through `gpu.rs`) already read
`spike_in` and wrote `spike_out` separately and swapped per launch, so the Orin,
4090 and CPU bench-*kernel* figures are unchanged. `bench --device cpu` and
`loop --device cpu` were the affected paths; they now run the synchronous
recurrence. The committed host CPU figure (20.615 ms/tick) is a timing, not a
correctness claim, and is unchanged.
